15 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Diagram depicting a tracked vehicle with labeled vectors and axes. The vehicle is on an XY coordinate plane, tilted at angle theta. Vectors V_L and V_R represent left and right track velocities in green. V_C is the vehicle's center velocity, and omega is its rotational velocity. The red vector shows the y-axis direction, and the blue dashed line represents the x-axis. Dimension b indicates the distance between the tracks.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Flowchart depicting a control system for vehicle navigation. It starts with vehicle state prediction, motor controller feedback, and current vehicle state. The process includes computations for left/right track slip ratios and employs an improved particle swarm optimization algorithm. It features fuzzy control for look-ahead distance and analyzes average path curvature. The target-point position is corrected continuously. If the target is not reached, a pure pursuit controller and low-pass filter for velocity commands are used, leading to chassis control. The process repeats until the target is reached, concluding the cycle.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating a path-following model with axes labeled X and Y. The vehicle, denoted by a rectangle, is at point C. It follows a curved path ending at point t and target point tp. The path includes dashed and solid lines, with angles and distances labeled, such as \\\(L_d\\\) and \\\(\\delta\\\). A reference path is also indicated.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 55 0 R /Alt (Three line graphs display membership functions. The first graph shows the function for input curvature \(k\) with small, medium, and large categories. The second graph represents input velocity \(v\) with similar categories. The third graph depicts the function for output look-ahead distance \(Ld\), categorized as very small, small, medium, large, and very large, with overlapping triangular and trapezoidal shapes across each x-axis.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt (Three-dimensional graph showing the output response surface of a fuzzy controller. The axes are labeled v \(Velocity\), k \(Path curvature\), and Ld \(Look-ahead distance\). The surface varies in color from blue to yellow, indicating different response values.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 57 0 R /Alt (Graph comparing inertia-weight decay strategies in Particle Swarm Optimization \(PSO\). It shows the number of iterations against inertia weight, with two lines: a solid line for \215Number of iterations\216 and a dashed line for \215Nonlinear Chebyshev-window type \(K = 0.9\),\216 both decreasing from 0.9 to 0.4 over 100 iterations.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 58 0 R /Alt (Panel a shows a plot of Y versus X in meters, with a smooth, undulating curve. Panel b displays a graph of curvature in per meter against path point index, featuring blue peaks of varying heights. Panel c illustrates the variation of slip ratios for left and right tracks and overall, depicted in different colored step lines over path length in meters.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 59 0 R /Alt (Graph titled \215Path tracking comparison\216 showing three trajectories: a black reference path, a blue fuzzy pure pursuit trajectory, and a red trajectory with offset compensation. A magnified section highlights the differences between the trajectories, particularly in a curve around x = 35. X-axis and Y-axis run from 0 to 45 and -20 to 20, respectively.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 60 0 R /Alt (Graph showing offset angle variation over time steps on a degree scale. The blue line fluctuates, displaying initial sharp decline followed by oscillations, peaking around 175 and 275, with minimal point near 250. The x-axis represents time steps, and the y-axis represents angle in degrees.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 61 0 R /Alt (Tracked vehicle with a mounted fan and tanks in panel a, part of a firefighting robot system. Panel b shows a block diagram of the integrated navigation system, including components like DTU-100, FSS-NAV680-AG, industrial computer, SG121-BGCM, and DBMCA2, communicating with the chassis control system and RS485 motor controller for left and right track motors.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 62 0 R /Alt (A robotic farming machine with red and yellow components is positioned on a plowed field. The device includes a large red wheel and tracks for mobility. A row of trees and buildings are visible in the background.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 46 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 63 0 R /Alt (Graphs show reference versus actual trajectories. Chart \(a\) and \(b\) feature similar elliptical paths with variations. Blue lines depict fuzzy pure pursuit trajectories, red lines show trajectories with offset compensation, and black lines represent reference paths. Each graph includes enlarged sections highlighting differences in trajectory paths.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 47 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Two line graphs compare lateral deviation over travel distance for two speeds: 0.35 meters per second and 0.75 meters per second. Blue lines represent fuzzy pure pursuit, and red lines represent offset compensation. The graphs show variations in lateral deviation, with offset compensation consistently resulting in lower deviations compared to fuzzy pure pursuit. Each graph has the same axes labeled \215Lateral deviation/m\216 for the y-axis and \215Travel distance/m\216 for the x-axis.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 48 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
